---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCode from "../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "TUI Entry", href: "#tui-entry", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Worker Model", href: "#worker-model", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Companion UI", href: "#companion-ui", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Remote Control", href: "#remote-control", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Attach", href: "#attach", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="TUI"
  subtitle="Terminal UI entrypoint and worker model in packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="tui-entry" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>TUI Entry</h2>
    <p>
      The TUI is the default CLI route. It spawns a worker thread and communicates with it over an
      RPC channel. The entrypoint is <code>packages/nikcli/src/cli/cmd/tui/thread.ts</code> and the
      command is registered as <code>$0 [project]</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Local vs server mode">
      <p>
        The TUI can run fully locally or connect to an existing nikcli server via network flags.
        When <code>--server</code> flags are present, the TUI boots an HTTP server and uses events
        to stream state to connected clients.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="worker-model" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Worker Model</h2>
    <p>
      The worker process runs the session engine, agent loops, tool execution, and permission
      evaluation off the main thread. The main thread handles UI rendering and user I/O.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="companion-ui" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Companion UI</h2>
    <p>
      nikcli ships a web-based companion UI that runs in the browser and connects to the same
      session stream as the TUI.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
      nikcli companion serve
      nikcli companion open
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      This is distinct from the mobile app; the companion UI targets desktop browser access to a
      nikcli session.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="remote-control" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Remote Control</h2>
    <p>
      Remote Control exposes a running nikcli session to remote clients over a tunneled URL. Inside
      a TUI session, use the <code>/remote-control</code> (alias <code>/rc</code>) slash command.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Slash commands">
      <p>
        Use <code>/remote-control</code> (or alias <code>/rc</code>) inside a TUI session to open the
        Remote Control dialog. Legacy alias <code>/remote</code> is still supported.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      If a remote session already exists for the running instance, nikcli reuses it and keeps the
      share URL in sync. Remote defaults are configurable under <code>config.remote</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="attach" class="mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <h2>Attach</h2>
    <p>
      <code>nikcli attach &lt;url&gt;</code> connects a local TUI to a shared session hosted by a
      remote nikcli server.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
